235 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Why does the SVD of a matrix reveal its rank?)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
236 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The Singular Value Decomposition \(SVD\) of a matrix reveals its rank because it decomposes the matrix into a series of singular values and corresponding singular vectors. The rank of a matrix is the maximum number of linearly independent columns \(or rows\) it has. In the SVD decomposition, each singular value corresponds to a specific singular vector \(set of linearly independent vectors\). The number of non-zero singular values in the decomposition corresponds to the number of these linearly independent vectors, hence revealing the rank of the matrix. Moreover, these singular values are presented in decreasing order, meaning the significant values \(non-zero\) come first and represent the rank of the original matrix. If a singular value is zero, it means there is a redundancy in the matrix's columns or rows.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
